WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.160
You know that feeling, right?

00:01.660 --> 00:06.260
When you're just sitting there staring at your computer screen and you have like 20,

00:06.400 --> 00:09.300
maybe 30 tabs open across three different browser windows.

00:09.520 --> 00:09.880
Oh, yeah.

00:10.140 --> 00:12.700
Or, you know, four different windows if it's a really bad day.

00:12.780 --> 00:13.300
Right, exactly.

00:13.520 --> 00:16.300
And you've got a stack of half-read books on your nightstand.

00:16.460 --> 00:21.500
You've got this notes app on your phone that is just overflowing with random ideas, quotes

00:21.500 --> 00:26.180
you liked, grocery lists, maybe some fragments of thoughts you had in the shower.

00:26.420 --> 00:28.100
It's a complete digital junk drawer.

00:28.320 --> 00:28.480
Yeah.

00:28.480 --> 00:34.660
And there's this profound, almost physical sensation of being utterly overwhelmed by

00:34.660 --> 00:34.960
it all.

00:35.440 --> 00:39.600
You look at all this information, all these pieces of your own life and your own curiosity,

00:39.600 --> 00:46.120
and you realize with this sinking feeling that your brain simply cannot hold all of this at

00:46.120 --> 00:46.360
once.

00:46.620 --> 00:48.460
I mean, you've basically outgrown your own mind.

00:48.460 --> 00:49.440
It's so true.

00:49.580 --> 00:55.140
And it feels like a uniquely modern anxiety, you know, driven by the internet and smartphones

00:55.140 --> 00:57.600
and having a world of data in our pockets.

00:57.600 --> 01:02.380
But structurally speaking, it's of a very old, very fundamental human problem.

01:02.480 --> 01:02.680
Really?

01:02.780 --> 01:04.160
Like, historically speaking?

01:04.480 --> 01:05.160
Oh, absolutely.

01:05.380 --> 01:08.080
We hit the upper limit of our working memory.

01:08.420 --> 01:12.560
Like, the absolute boundary of what we can hold in our unassisted cognition.

01:12.560 --> 01:16.880
And we are forced to build an external structure just to catch the overflow.

01:16.880 --> 01:20.620
We literally have to outsource our memory just to function in the world.

01:20.620 --> 01:24.220
And that is exactly the jumping off point for this deep dive today.

01:24.360 --> 01:27.440
We are exploring a truly fascinating paper.

01:27.640 --> 01:32.680
It's titled, Paracosms of the Strange, written by an independent researcher who goes by the

01:32.680 --> 01:34.640
name FLYXION.

01:34.860 --> 01:36.100
It's a brilliant piece of work.

01:36.200 --> 01:36.840
It really is.

01:36.840 --> 01:41.660
And our mission today, for you listening, is to look at what actually happens when we

01:41.660 --> 01:45.820
build these external systems, these archives, basically, to hold our excess thoughts.

01:46.140 --> 01:51.260
Because, as this paper argues so well, these systems don't just sit there like a dusty storage

01:51.260 --> 01:51.640
locker.

01:51.760 --> 01:51.940
Right.

01:52.020 --> 01:53.140
They aren't passive at all.

01:53.340 --> 01:53.740
Exactly.

01:53.900 --> 01:56.440
If you build them a certain way, they actually start thinking with you.

01:56.800 --> 01:57.060
Yeah.

01:57.060 --> 02:01.900
The core premise here is that the architecture of our external memory actively shapes what

02:01.900 --> 02:03.580
we are capable of thinking next.

02:04.260 --> 02:08.700
And to explore this, the paper examines three radically different historical figures who,

02:09.020 --> 02:11.200
honestly, have almost nothing in common.

02:11.560 --> 02:11.780
Right.

02:11.920 --> 02:13.300
They are from completely different worlds.

02:13.400 --> 02:13.880
Exactly.

02:14.220 --> 02:19.200
Not on the level of their personal beliefs, their professions, or even their historical eras.

02:19.300 --> 02:24.500
I mean, we have Nicholas Luhmann, who is a highly disciplined 20th century sociologist.

02:24.700 --> 02:25.640
Super academic guy.

02:25.800 --> 02:26.220
Very.

02:26.220 --> 02:34.300
Then we have Immanuel Swedenborg, an 18th century visionary and natural philosopher who, well,

02:34.720 --> 02:36.400
he claimed to converse with angels.

02:36.600 --> 02:37.380
As one does.

02:37.640 --> 02:37.900
Right.

02:38.180 --> 02:44.800
And finally, we have Philip K. Dick, the legendary science fiction novelist who is desperately trying

02:44.800 --> 02:50.900
to decipher this massive, mind-bending, anomalous experience he had in the 1970s.

02:51.020 --> 02:53.780
And I want to be incredibly clear with you right out of the gate here.

02:53.780 --> 02:57.740
We are not here to judge the truth of what these three men believed.

02:57.860 --> 02:58.600
No, not at all.

02:58.680 --> 03:03.080
We aren't here to decide if Swedenborg really talked to ghosts or if Philip K. Dick actually

03:03.080 --> 03:06.700
experienced contact with some, you know, transtemporal intelligence.

03:06.700 --> 03:10.320
What we are examining is their shared situation.

03:10.320 --> 03:13.920
Which is that they all experienced a massive cognitive overload.

03:13.920 --> 03:14.680
Exactly.

03:14.820 --> 03:20.100
They all had experiences or accumulated research that completely overwhelmed their unassisted memory.

03:20.100 --> 03:30.020
So they all built these massive, obsessive external architectures, basically filing systems, so they could return to their own thoughts later.

03:30.020 --> 03:40.440
And treating these three figures not as, you know, isolated eccentrics, but as rigorous case studies in how archives function, it reveals something profound.

03:40.560 --> 03:41.100
It really does.

03:41.240 --> 03:49.200
So by the end of this deep dive, we are going to uncover the hidden mathematical, structural, and even physical foundations of how knowledge is actually processed.

03:49.200 --> 03:56.620
We're going to see how memory, reality, and even entire alternate worlds are constructed simply by how we choose to file our thoughts.

03:56.740 --> 03:58.780
So let's start with the anatomy of the archive itself.

03:58.900 --> 04:03.840
Because building a system to hold our cognitive overflow, I mean, that isn't a new idea at all.

04:03.880 --> 04:04.740
Not even a little bit.

04:04.860 --> 04:09.580
The paper provides a really rift genealogy of how humans have externalized their minds throughout history.

04:09.760 --> 04:14.460
It goes way back to antiquity and the early modern period with something called commonplace books.

04:14.460 --> 04:23.420
Okay, yeah. I've actually heard this term thrown around in podativity circles today, but what did a commonplace book actually look like back then?

04:23.700 --> 04:28.700
Well, think of a commonplace book as the most direct ancestor of the systems we're going to be discussing.

04:29.180 --> 04:39.800
For centuries, spanning from thinkers like Seneca in ancient Rome up through Renaissance figures like Erasmus, readers and writers would keep these physical notebooks.

04:40.120 --> 04:41.420
So just like a regular journal?

04:41.700 --> 04:43.380
Sort of, but highly structured.

04:43.380 --> 04:53.680
When they encountered a striking passage in a text, a useful observation about agriculture, or a compelling philosophical quotation, they would copy it out by hand.

04:53.960 --> 04:57.980
But the crucial part is that they didn't just write it chronologically like a diary.

04:58.180 --> 04:59.640
Oh, I see. They organized it.

04:59.720 --> 05:05.340
Exactly. They filed it under specific, predetermined headings or topics that they had chosen in advance.

05:05.660 --> 05:08.740
Things like courage, betrayal, rhetoric, or statecraft.

05:09.080 --> 05:11.900
Ah, so it was a repository to be drawn upon later.

05:11.900 --> 05:24.720
Like, if you were a lawyer or a politician preparing a speech about loyalty, you would flip to the loyalty section of your commonplace book, and you'd have this curated list of the best quotes from Aristotle or Cicero right there.

05:24.900 --> 05:26.080
Right. It was highly functional.

05:26.660 --> 05:27.860
A very practical tool.

05:28.100 --> 05:28.220
Yeah.

05:28.220 --> 05:33.860
And we see this same impulse outside of secular reading, too. The paper mentions the floriligium.

05:34.480 --> 05:35.740
Floriligium. That's a great word.

05:35.840 --> 05:44.660
It literally translates from Latin as a gathering of flowers. In the medieval period, these were used for compiling excerpts from patristic sources.

05:44.960 --> 05:48.340
Patristic sources meaning, like, the early church fathers.

05:48.520 --> 05:48.720
Yeah.

05:49.160 --> 05:51.220
The foundational theologians of Christianity.

05:51.220 --> 06:07.360
Yes, exactly. The writings of figures like Augustine or Jerome were just vast and incredibly dense. A floriligium allowed a monk or a scholar to extract the flowers, you know, the most beautiful or theologically potent sentences, and organize them thematically.

06:07.680 --> 06:09.980
That makes total sense. Like an ancient highlight reel.

06:10.300 --> 06:14.280
And then you have the scholarly concordance, which takes us a massy step further.

06:14.400 --> 06:16.180
Right. I've seen concordances for the Bible.

06:16.180 --> 06:30.300
Yeah. A concordance is an exhaustive alphabetical index of every single time a word or phrase appears in a mass of text. It was a monumental human achievement to compile these before computers.

06:30.660 --> 06:33.080
I can't even imagine the manual labor involved in that.

06:33.180 --> 06:42.380
It's staggering. But they exist because the text is simply too large and interconnected for anyone, no matter how devout or brilliant, to hold perfectly in their working memory.

06:42.380 --> 06:51.620
But it wasn't just text either, which I found fascinating. The paper brings up A.B. Warburg's Nemeson Atlas from the 1920s, which is just wild to think about.

06:51.720 --> 06:54.680
Oh, the Nemeson Atlas is incredible. It was a visual archive.

06:54.940 --> 07:00.480
Right. Warburg was an art historian, and he used these massive wooden frames covered in black cloth.

07:01.020 --> 07:08.500
He would take photographs, reproductions of classical art, images from contemporary newspapers, and physically pin them to the cloth.

07:08.500 --> 07:19.600
Yeah. He was tracing the survival and transformation of classical motifs, what he called pathos formulas, or expressive gestures across wildly different historical periods and cultures.

07:19.840 --> 07:22.460
So he'd have a classical statue next to a newspaper clipping.

07:22.840 --> 07:27.340
Exactly. By pinning them on these boards, he could constantly rearrange them.

07:27.740 --> 07:37.500
It was a way of externalizing association, letting images sit next to each other to reveal visual relationships that a written linear narrative would just obscure.

07:37.500 --> 07:41.920
Basically, a giant analog mood board for art history.

07:42.120 --> 07:43.740
That is a perfect way to describe it.

07:43.820 --> 07:51.000
But there's also a social equivalent to this externalized memory that the paper touches on, the medieval marginalia in glosses.

07:51.080 --> 07:53.480
Oh, right, writing in the margins of books.

07:53.720 --> 07:58.820
Yes. But in medieval scriptoria, books were rare and incredibly valuable.

07:58.820 --> 08:06.660
You have these heavily annotated manuscripts where the commentary wasn't written by just one person over decades, sometimes centuries.

08:07.200 --> 08:11.960
Successive readers would write their own notes in the margins or between the lines, which is what a gloss is.

08:12.100 --> 08:14.300
So they were kind of talking to each other across time.

08:14.560 --> 08:15.040
Exactly.

08:15.040 --> 08:20.980
They were responding not just to the original text, but to the notes left by the readers who came before them.

08:21.560 --> 08:23.800
The archive accreted across a community.

08:24.240 --> 08:35.160
No single person planned the layout of those notes, but it became this layered topographical record of many minds encountering the same text from many different later positions in time.

08:35.160 --> 08:41.940
But here is the critical distinction that paper makes, and we really need to spend some time on this because it's the pivot point for our entire discussion today.

08:42.320 --> 08:50.320
All those older systems, the commonplace books, the florilegia, the concordances, they are what the author calls categorical.

08:50.480 --> 08:54.280
Yes. They are organized by fixed, pre-decided targets.

08:54.360 --> 08:59.880
Right. But the modern archive that we are focusing on with our three main figures is combinatorial.

08:59.880 --> 09:05.440
The distinction is absolutely vital. Categorical systems are top-down. Commentorial systems are bottom-up.

09:05.520 --> 09:10.240
Okay. Let's unpack this for everyone listening. I like to think of a commonplace book as a grocery store.

09:10.400 --> 09:11.440
A grocery store? Okay.

09:11.700 --> 09:18.300
Yeah. Where everything is in its designated aisle. You go to aisle four for the pasta. You go to aisle seven for the cereal.

09:18.580 --> 09:23.640
The categories are locked. The manager decided where the pasta goes before you even walked in the door.

09:23.840 --> 09:26.660
If you are looking for pasta, you know exactly where to go.

09:26.740 --> 09:27.600
Right. It's predictable.

09:27.600 --> 09:41.460
Exactly. But a combinatorial archive is, well, it's more like a chaotic cocktail party where any two ideas, no matter how unrelated they seem on the surface, can just bump into each other and start a completely unexpected conversation.

09:41.780 --> 09:52.540
That analogy perfectly captures the difference in retrieval. In a categorical system like your grocery store, retrieval only returns material relevant to a category you already had in your mind.

09:52.540 --> 09:52.960
Right.

09:53.060 --> 10:02.220
You go looking for pasta. You find pasta. You go looking for quotes on courage. You find quotes on courage. The system answers your question, but it doesn't change your question.

10:02.440 --> 10:03.620
That is a great way to put it.

10:03.720 --> 10:13.400
A combinatorial system, however, has an addressing logic that allows any single entry to stand in relation to any other entry, regardless of the overarching topic.

10:13.400 --> 10:23.360
But wait, let me push back on this a little bit. Isn't an archive, at the end of the day, really just a memory prosthetic? Like, isn't it just an external hard drive for a human being?

10:23.620 --> 10:25.100
A lot of people think of it that way, yeah.

10:25.300 --> 10:34.180
Right. I write something down so I don't have to spend the mental energy remembering it. And when I need it later, I just query the system and download it back into my brain.

10:34.180 --> 10:46.800
It is incredibly tempting to view our notes that way, especially now that we literally use hard drives to store them. But the paper argues fiercely against this hard drive concept of human archiving.

10:46.900 --> 10:47.860
Because it's too passive.

10:48.000 --> 10:57.480
Exactly. Storage is inert. A locked drawer stores things perfectly well. A hard drive just holds data exactly as you left it, as a string of ones and zeros.

10:57.480 --> 11:07.800
An archive, a true combinatorial archive used by a human being, is active. It is dynamic. The key concept the paper introduces here is recontextualization.

11:08.940 --> 11:11.100
Recontextualization, meaning the context literally changes.

11:11.160 --> 11:20.240
Yes. The paper represents this mathematically with the operator, wrote the Greek letter. Think about what happens when you retrieve a journal entry or a note you wrote, say, five years ago.

11:20.500 --> 11:23.340
Okay, I'm thinking about it. Usually I cringe.

11:23.340 --> 11:37.320
Right. Because you are not just recalling an old inscription like a computer loading a file into RAM. You are experiencing an encounter. It is a collision between that old note and the new present state of your mind.

11:37.460 --> 11:40.520
Because I'm literally not the same person I was five years ago.

11:40.800 --> 11:52.920
Precisely. You have read different books. You have had different relationships. You have suffered different failures and achieved different successes. You have formed entirely new questions about the world.

11:52.920 --> 11:54.120
That makes a lot of sense.

11:54.480 --> 12:07.700
Therefore, the result of reading that old note today is a completely new cognitive state. It generates a thought that did not exist when the note was originally written, and it is a thought you could not have had today if you hadn't collided with your past self.

12:07.920 --> 12:11.540
Oh, wow. So it's not a retrieval at all. It's a chemical reaction.

12:11.780 --> 12:12.040
Yeah.

12:12.200 --> 12:16.120
Two separate elements combining to make something completely volatile and new.

12:16.120 --> 12:26.700
Exactly. And if we look at Appendix F in the paper, it introduces this beautiful mathematical concept to explain this chemical reaction. It says that interpretation is noncommutative.

12:27.440 --> 12:37.820
Noncommutative, Kate. Like in basic math, addition is commutative. Three plus four is seven, and four plus three is seven. The order doesn't matter. So noncommutative means the order absolutely changes the result.

12:37.820 --> 12:49.640
Right. Matrix multiplication is noncommutative. In our context, reading note A and then note B produces a completely different intellectual state in your mind than if you had read note B and then note A.

12:49.760 --> 12:52.120
Okay. Give me an example of how that works in practice.

12:52.120 --> 12:58.300
Imagine note A is a historical account of a war, and note B is a psychological theory about trauma.

12:58.420 --> 12:58.720
Got it.

12:58.720 --> 13:18.640
If you read the history first, then the psychology, you might analyze the historical figures through a psychological lens. But if you read the psychology first and then the history, the historical events might serve to completely validate or invalidate the psychological theory you just absorbed. The topology and the path of return matter as much as the contents.

13:18.640 --> 13:35.860
So the archive isn't just a static collection of facts. The path you take through your own notes actually changes you. And because your mind is changed, the way you search the archive the next time will be different, which changes what you find. It is this intense, recursive, dynamic loop.

13:36.060 --> 13:45.740
Which brings us to our first major case study, Nicholas Luhmann. He is the ultimate example of this dynamic loop in action, and he built a physical machine to facilitate it.

13:45.740 --> 13:48.320
Luhmann was a 20th century German sociologist, right?

13:48.460 --> 13:58.740
Yes. He essentially reinvented sociological systems theory. But for our purposes today, what makes him legendary is his zettelkasten, which translates to slipbox.

13:59.100 --> 14:04.940
And we should clarify, this wasn't just a little shoebox on his desk. This was a massive physical piece of furniture.

14:05.040 --> 14:05.740
Oh, huge.

14:05.740 --> 14:14.260
We are talking about custom-built wooden cabinets holding roughly 90,000 individual index cards accumulated over decades of his academic career.

14:14.380 --> 14:20.880
Yeah, and FLYXion deliberately chooses Luhmann as our control case for this entire deep dive. It is vital that we start with him.

14:21.040 --> 14:24.500
Why is that? Why start with the sociologist instead of the sci-fi writer?

14:24.500 --> 14:35.760
Because Luhmann's notes are entirely mundane. He is writing about the sociology of trust, the evolution of contract law, public administration, and something called double contingency in systems theory.

14:36.180 --> 14:43.540
Which admittedly sounds incredibly dry. Before we move on, what is double contingency? Just so we have a flavor of what he was actually thinking about.

14:43.540 --> 14:49.440
Sure. In sociology, double contingency refers to the fundamental problem of any social interaction.

14:50.240 --> 14:56.720
Imagine two people meeting for the first time. Person A's actions depend on what they think Person B will do.

14:56.920 --> 14:58.040
Okay, like a standoff.

14:58.160 --> 15:04.120
Right. But Person B's actions depend on what they think Person A will do. It's a hall of mirrors.

15:04.960 --> 15:09.960
How does society ever form if everyone is constantly waiting for the other person to act first?

15:10.060 --> 15:11.440
Oh, that's actually really interesting.

15:11.440 --> 15:19.580
Right. Luhmann studied how social systems like law or the economy or mass media develop rules to solve this paralysis.

15:20.200 --> 15:27.380
Fascinating, but definitely not supernatural. There is zero metaphysical, supernatural, or anomalous charge to this material.

15:28.100 --> 15:31.200
He isn't talking to ghosts. He's analyzing societal structures.

15:31.200 --> 15:43.980
And that is the point. If this combinatorial magic, this chemical reaction of notes we've been talking about, only happened to mystics and science fiction writers, we could just write it off as them being, well, mystical and strange.

15:44.060 --> 15:45.360
We just say, oh, they're just crazy.

15:45.580 --> 15:50.720
Exactly. But Luhmann is a rigorous, highly disciplined academic.

15:50.720 --> 15:59.000
By establishing how this archival mechanism works on material no one would call visionary, the paper shifts the burden of explanation.

15:59.340 --> 16:00.020
Oh, I see.

16:00.020 --> 16:12.980
If Luhmann's totally mundane sociology archive can generate wild, unexpected surprises, then when we look at the strange archives of our visionary figures later, we can't just blame the weirdness on their visions.

16:13.220 --> 16:16.080
We have to look at the architecture of the archive itself.

16:16.440 --> 16:18.620
So let's get into the mechanics of that architecture.

16:18.800 --> 16:18.980
Yeah.

16:18.980 --> 16:23.980
How did he physically organize 90,000 cards without using the grocery store aisles?

16:24.480 --> 16:28.240
Well, the addressing logic of Luhmann's slipbox was totally non-hierarchical.

16:28.400 --> 16:31.160
He didn't have a folder for economics and a folder for law.

16:31.220 --> 16:32.160
How did he find anything then?

16:32.160 --> 16:32.340
Yeah.

16:32.680 --> 16:38.160
Instead, he numbered his cards based purely on their relative position to the card that came right before them.

16:39.020 --> 16:41.520
When he had a thought, he would write it on a card.

16:41.640 --> 16:42.800
Let's say it's card number one.

16:42.920 --> 16:43.900
Okay. Simple on that.

16:43.900 --> 16:48.000
If he had another thought that built directly on that, it became card two.

16:48.000 --> 16:55.080
But what if, a year later, he was reviewing card one and had a tangential thought, a new branch?

16:55.180 --> 16:57.620
He couldn't make it card two because card two already existed.

16:57.800 --> 16:59.300
Right. So he made a card 1A.

16:59.460 --> 17:03.500
And if he had a thought stemming from 1A, it became 1A1, then 1A1A.

17:03.560 --> 17:07.140
Exactly. This allowed for infinite internal branching.

17:07.700 --> 17:14.360
A single card could sprout new lines of thought in any direction forever without disrupting the physical order of the box.

17:14.420 --> 17:15.720
That is genuinely brilliant.

17:15.720 --> 17:17.980
But the real magic was the cross-referencing.

17:18.000 --> 17:22.500
Because the cards were permanently fixed with these unique alphanumeric addresses,

17:22.940 --> 17:27.820
he could write a note on card 450B that just said, see also card 1A1.

17:27.820 --> 17:32.500
This addressing scheme means any card can be reached from multiple different pathways.

17:32.760 --> 17:39.000
A note on the sociology of trust, which might have been filed as a branch off a card about contract law,

17:39.360 --> 17:45.340
might later be reached by you when you are following a completely different branch line about intimate personal relationships.

17:45.340 --> 17:49.840
The context completely shifts based on the path you took to get there.

17:49.840 --> 17:51.840
And here is where it gets really interesting.

17:51.840 --> 17:57.160
And it's the profound paradox of Lumen's system, which the paper highlights beautifully.

17:58.040 --> 18:02.280
For Lumen, a finished archive would be a failed archive.

18:02.500 --> 18:03.960
That seems counterintuitive.

18:04.160 --> 18:06.700
We usually think of research as driving toward a conclusion.

18:06.700 --> 18:10.240
You gather your notes to write the definitive book on a topic, and then you're done.

18:10.240 --> 18:15.400
But Lumen's system was explicitly designed for what the author calls sustained non-convergence.

18:16.040 --> 18:21.760
It was built to never settle on a final answer or a grand concluding theory of everything.

18:21.880 --> 18:22.700
It just keeps going.

18:22.700 --> 18:25.540
It was designed to constantly generate divergence.

18:26.360 --> 18:29.980
There is no privileged central object it is trying to interpret.

18:30.640 --> 18:36.140
The entire value of the system lies in its openness to being reached from interpretive positions

18:36.140 --> 18:38.540
that haven't even been invented yet by the author.

18:38.740 --> 18:43.240
It's like a machine built purely to generate endless aha moments.

18:43.780 --> 18:49.080
And the paper actually backs this up with some serious math in the appendices to prove it's not just a metaphor.

18:49.080 --> 18:53.840
Let's look at Appendix B, which delves into associative distance and geometry.

18:54.100 --> 18:57.180
Yeah, the paper argues that when Lumen added a cross-reference,

18:57.460 --> 19:00.360
when he wrote C-card 1A1 on a distant card,

19:00.600 --> 19:04.020
he wasn't actually adding new informational content to the box.

19:04.200 --> 19:05.000
What is he doing then?

19:05.260 --> 19:07.760
What he was doing was changing the geometry of the archive.

19:08.160 --> 19:13.760
He was mathematically shortening the path required for a future version of himself to reach an old idea.

19:13.920 --> 19:14.460
Oh, wow.

19:14.460 --> 19:19.440
It's like building a wormhole between two completely different galaxies in the universe of your notes.

19:20.000 --> 19:24.040
The paper makes a brilliant distinction here between preservation and reachability.

19:24.860 --> 19:27.600
A preserved note that takes infinite steps to reach,

19:27.860 --> 19:31.460
or requires you to remember exactly what day you wrote it 10 years ago to find it,

19:31.760 --> 19:33.220
is functionally absent.

19:33.500 --> 19:34.240
It's useless.

19:34.580 --> 19:34.780
Right.

19:34.840 --> 19:35.980
You might as well have never written it.

19:36.080 --> 19:36.480
Exactly.

19:37.200 --> 19:39.240
An effective archive doesn't just store.

19:39.240 --> 19:45.280
It creates and shortens the paths by which old thoughts become active inputs for new cognition.

19:45.740 --> 19:49.200
So it's about the shape of the web, not just the number of flies caught in it.

19:49.320 --> 19:54.540
And then there's Appendix D, which looks at this through information theory and the concept of surprise.

19:55.100 --> 19:58.720
This gets a bit dense, mentioning the Kolbeck-Leibler divergence.

19:59.500 --> 20:01.660
How do we translate that into human terms?

20:01.660 --> 20:02.300
Right.

20:02.460 --> 20:10.280
So Kolbeck-Leibler divergence is a measure of how one probability distribution differs from a second reference probability distribution.

20:11.020 --> 20:18.660
In the context of information theory, it measures the amount of surprise or new information you gain when you update your beliefs based on new evidence.

20:18.980 --> 20:19.220
Okay.

20:19.320 --> 20:20.720
So applied to the slip box.

20:20.960 --> 20:21.680
How does that look?

20:21.940 --> 20:22.940
The paper asks,

20:22.940 --> 20:28.520
How does an inanimate box of wooden drawers and paper cards act like a communication partner?

20:29.140 --> 20:30.100
It's not magic.

20:30.600 --> 20:36.780
It happens because the archive's retrieval behavior systematically departs from what the reader's unaided mind would expect.

20:37.280 --> 20:37.560
Okay.

20:37.740 --> 20:39.800
Let's imagine a spectrum of archives.

20:40.340 --> 20:43.940
On one end, you have an archive that gives you exactly what you expected.

20:44.280 --> 20:46.280
You ask for quotes on courage.

20:46.280 --> 20:49.640
It gives you your three favorite quotes on courage.

20:49.940 --> 20:50.960
That is a perfect mirror.

20:51.120 --> 20:51.340
Right.

20:51.340 --> 20:52.840
It is totally redundant.

20:53.120 --> 20:57.540
It has zero KL divergence, and it is incredibly boring.

20:57.820 --> 20:59.540
It doesn't push your thinking forward.

20:59.660 --> 21:01.060
It just tells you what you already know.

21:01.180 --> 21:06.980
Now imagine the other end of the spectrum, an archive that gives you totally random, unrelated cards.

21:07.420 --> 21:11.580
You look up a note on sociology, and it hands you a grocery list from 1982.

21:11.980 --> 21:12.500
Okay, yeah.

21:12.520 --> 21:13.680
That is pure noise.

21:13.800 --> 21:19.500
It has massive divergence, but it's useless because you can't integrate it into your current thought process.

21:19.500 --> 21:19.720
Right.

21:19.780 --> 21:20.920
It's just chaotic nonsense.

21:21.160 --> 21:21.840
It's too surprising.

21:22.260 --> 21:22.740
Exactly.

21:23.440 --> 21:29.480
Lumen's archive, through its complex branching and cross-referencing, occupies the sweet spot in between.

21:30.120 --> 21:40.360
It returns material that is intelligible to your current state of mind, because you followed a logical trail of links to get there, but independent enough to alter your state of mind.

21:40.360 --> 21:43.140
It generates genuine cognitive surprise.

21:43.280 --> 21:43.520
Yes.

21:43.520 --> 21:53.020
You pull a thread on legal theory, and through a cross-reference you made years ago and forgot about, you suddenly find yourself reading a note about the biology of trust.

21:53.460 --> 21:55.580
It forces a new chemical reaction.

21:55.800 --> 21:59.100
I think about this like the algorithm on Spotify Discover Weekly.

21:59.300 --> 22:00.420
Oh, that's a good comparison.

22:00.420 --> 22:02.200
Yeah, if it just plays my favorite songs, I get bored.

22:02.760 --> 22:05.640
If it plays random death metal when I want jazz, I turn it off.

22:06.320 --> 22:14.720
But when it plays a jazz track with a weird electronic beat I've never heard before, but that perfectly matches my current mood, that is the sweet spot.

22:14.840 --> 22:16.680
That is cognitive surprise.

22:16.820 --> 22:18.320
Okay, so that is our baseline.

22:18.320 --> 22:26.980
An ordinary, rigorous scholar using a combinatorial archive to generate an infinite, never-ending stream of surprising connections.

22:27.400 --> 22:39.500
Now, we take that exact same architectural mechanism, the row operator, the encounters, the cross-referencing, the non-communativity, and we apply it to someone dealing with highly anomalous visionary material.

22:40.020 --> 22:41.320
This is where things get wild.

22:41.800 --> 22:44.780
What happens when the inputs aren't sociology but hallucinations?

22:45.760 --> 22:47.680
This brings us to Emanuel Swedenborg.

22:47.680 --> 22:51.700
Emanuel Swedenborg is one of the most fascinating figures in European history.

22:52.020 --> 22:55.960
We are talking about an 18th century Swedish polymath.

22:56.140 --> 22:57.360
A real Renaissance man, right?

22:57.480 --> 22:57.880
Completely.

22:58.180 --> 23:02.380
For the first half of his life, he was a brilliant, highly respected scientist and engineer.

23:02.620 --> 23:05.680
He made contributions to metallurgy, anatomy, and physics.

23:05.920 --> 23:07.980
He mapped the human cerebral cortex.

23:08.580 --> 23:11.020
He drafted plans for submarines and flying machines.

23:11.520 --> 23:13.380
He was an enlightenment man of science.

23:13.380 --> 23:21.400
But then, in his mid-50s, he goes through an intense psychological crisis and begins having profound, daily visionary experiences.

23:21.720 --> 23:24.180
He claimed that his spiritual eyes had been opened.

23:24.760 --> 23:32.320
He reported conversing with angels, with demons, with the spirits of deceased historical figures, and even with the inhabitants of other planets.

23:32.320 --> 23:37.080
And because he was a scientist accustomed to keeping rigorous notes, he didn't just have these visions.

23:37.520 --> 23:39.060
He recorded them meticulously.

23:39.280 --> 23:39.800
Meticulously.

23:40.400 --> 23:56.060
We are talking about decades of private records in his spiritual diary and his massive published theological work, the Arcana Caledestia, which was an exhaustive, cross-reference, scriptural exegesis of the books of Genesis and Exodus.

23:56.060 --> 23:58.880
And here is the million-dollar question the paper poses.

23:59.840 --> 24:08.100
What happens when you feed decades of wild, unpredictable, anomalous visions into the exact same archival mechanism that Lumen used?

24:08.160 --> 24:09.520
Right. Does it behave the same way?

24:09.680 --> 24:12.520
Does it diverge infinitely, like Lumen's sociology notes?

24:12.760 --> 24:15.720
Does it keep surprising him with weirder and weirder contradictions?

24:15.920 --> 24:16.480
It does not.

24:16.600 --> 24:18.160
In fact, it does the exact opposite.

24:18.680 --> 24:21.440
Instead of diverging, Swedenborg's archive converged.

24:21.640 --> 24:24.380
This is where I need to make sure I actually understand the mechanics.

24:24.380 --> 24:37.660
If he's applying the exact same mechanism as Lumen, he's recontextualizing, he's cross-referencing new notes with old notes, why does one explode with new questions and the other lock into a fixed universe?

24:38.020 --> 24:39.100
That's a great question.

24:39.400 --> 24:47.520
It sounds like Swedenborg accidentally built a gravity well that pulled all his crazy experiences into a single, inescapable orbit.

24:47.960 --> 24:50.880
Gravity well is the perfect description of what happened.

24:50.880 --> 25:00.820
The repeated application of the row operator recontextualizing new visions against the accumulated archive of older visions pushed the system toward a fixed point.

25:01.100 --> 25:04.740
It created a stabilized, traversable cosmology.

25:04.960 --> 25:06.700
How does that actually work in practice, though?

25:07.100 --> 25:11.340
Imagine Swedenborg has a chaotic, terrifying vision of a spirit.

25:11.740 --> 25:12.560
He writes it down.

25:12.940 --> 25:15.280
The next day, he has a slightly different vision.

25:15.280 --> 25:20.660
He writes it down, and he cross-references it with the first one, trying to find the underlying rule.

25:21.220 --> 25:26.060
He applies his scientific mind to classifying angels the way he used to classify minerals.

25:26.220 --> 25:26.600
Oh, I see.

25:26.800 --> 25:28.940
He's treating the anomalous as data to be sorted.

25:29.280 --> 25:39.340
Over years, this massive, potentially incoherent body of daily hallucinations begins to stabilize into a single, highly coherent world system.

25:39.340 --> 25:47.800
When a new, bizarre, visionary episode arrives ten years later, it is no longer left as a free-floating anomaly that shocked him.

25:48.200 --> 25:49.100
It's immediately absorbed.

25:49.220 --> 25:49.580
Exactly.

25:50.060 --> 25:54.080
It's interpreted using the massive framework established by the earlier episodes.

25:54.420 --> 25:56.640
The archive dictates the interpretation.

25:57.340 --> 26:03.180
Any region of his spiritual universe, whether it's the specific heaven of the ancient Greeks or the hell of the deceitful,

26:03.700 --> 26:07.540
could be reached from any other region using a shared interpretive key.

26:07.540 --> 26:10.880
But the paper points out a massive danger in this kind of stabilization.

26:11.200 --> 26:12.920
It calls it the vulnerability of convergence.

26:13.280 --> 26:19.440
Because a system that perfectly explains everything is incredibly dangerous if it's disconnected from reality.

26:19.580 --> 26:19.960
Yes.

26:20.340 --> 26:25.320
To understand the danger, we have to look closely at how Swedenborg's archive handles different types of information.

26:26.000 --> 26:33.580
In his spiritual diary, he frequently reports having long, detailed conversations with deceased historical figures, kings, philosophers, popes.

26:33.580 --> 26:40.840
Now, when those figures show up in his visions, they almost always act exactly the way historical texts say they should act.

26:40.960 --> 26:41.140
Right.

26:41.320 --> 26:45.360
If the ghost of King David shows up, he acts like a repentant warrior king.

26:45.860 --> 26:49.360
If Aristotle shows up, he wants to debate logic.

26:49.360 --> 26:51.800
The paper calls this a primed sheath.

26:52.620 --> 26:55.740
The archive isn't generating these personalities out of thin air.

26:56.140 --> 27:04.420
It is recombining an independently checkable store of associations that Swedenborg already had in his physical brain from reading history books.

27:04.580 --> 27:06.560
Because he was a highly educated man.

27:06.700 --> 27:07.240
Exactly.

27:07.640 --> 27:10.980
Because the vision matches the history book, it feels incredibly plausible.

27:11.520 --> 27:18.440
The archive's internal logic aligns with external historical reality, providing a powerful sense of verification to the author.

27:18.440 --> 27:21.560
You think, wow, this spirit knows exactly what Aristotle wrote.

27:21.660 --> 27:23.060
It must really be Aristotle.

27:23.440 --> 27:26.720
But really, it's just your own memory of Aristotle feeding the vision.

27:27.020 --> 27:29.580
But then we look at another of his books, Earths and the Universe.

27:30.220 --> 27:42.500
In this book, Swedenborg describes an exhaustive anthropological detail, the living conditions, the clothing, the religious beliefs, and the facial features of beings living on Mercury, Jupiter, Mars, and the moon.

27:42.500 --> 27:52.120
And at the time he was writing in the mid-1700s, there was absolutely no external record, no telescope, no scientific instrument capable of checking or verifying this.

27:52.320 --> 27:55.420
It was entirely unmoored from external evidence.

27:55.700 --> 27:57.060
And here's the terrifying part.

27:57.720 --> 28:01.500
The archive treats both of these experiences exactly the same.

28:01.580 --> 28:11.640
Meaning, the archival machinery processes the verifiable memory of a history book with the exact same authority as a completely uncheckable hallucination about moon map.

28:11.640 --> 28:12.320
Exactly.

28:13.120 --> 28:17.360
Appendix E formalizes this as the problem of epistemic identifiability.

28:17.840 --> 28:20.860
It's a concept drawn from statistics and econometrics.

28:21.440 --> 28:28.660
A system is unidentified if there is no way to distinguish between two different underlying realities based purely on the data the system produces.

28:28.920 --> 28:29.880
Okay, I think I follow.

28:29.880 --> 28:33.440
A knowledge system can achieve perfect internal coherence.

28:33.960 --> 28:38.200
Every single note in Swedenborg's diary can cross-reference perfectly with every other note.

28:38.520 --> 28:42.940
The theology of the Jupiterians might align flawlessly with the theology he discussed with King David.

28:43.580 --> 28:46.900
But internal consistency does not equal external truth.

28:47.320 --> 28:50.600
A beautifully written fantasy novel is internally consistent.

28:51.040 --> 28:51.480
Exactly.

28:51.940 --> 28:53.540
Two completely different hypotheses.

28:54.400 --> 28:55.380
Hypothesis A.

28:55.380 --> 28:58.780
God is revealing the true structure of the universe to me.

28:59.380 --> 29:00.220
Hypothesis B.

29:00.660 --> 29:07.580
I am suffering from a complex, systematized delusion can generate the exact same internal archival predictions.

29:07.580 --> 29:22.980
Once Swedenborg's archive stabilized into that fixed point, that gravity well, it offered no internal marker whatsoever to distinguish between a vision that was primed by a history book and a vision that was spun purely from the prime sheath of his own uncheckable imagination.

29:22.980 --> 29:25.420
The consistency itself becomes a trap.

29:25.740 --> 29:27.080
The machine works too well.

29:27.480 --> 29:31.720
It smooths over any anomaly by forcing it to fit the existing structure.

29:31.920 --> 29:38.580
It's like building a beautifully intricate, structurally flawless house of cards, but forgetting to check if you build it on solid rock or quicksand.

29:38.840 --> 29:40.720
The house itself looks perfect from the inside.

29:40.840 --> 29:41.660
The door is all open.

29:41.800 --> 29:42.780
The roof doesn't leak.

29:43.620 --> 29:46.260
But the foundation is entirely detached from the world.

29:46.540 --> 29:48.500
That is a brilliant way to conceptualize it.

29:48.500 --> 29:53.860
Okay, so we've seen infinite divergence with Luhmann, where the machine constantly generates new questions.

29:53.860 --> 30:01.140
And we've seen terminal convergence with Swedenborg, where the machine locks down into a fixed, inescapable cosmology.

30:01.860 --> 30:09.980
That brings us to our third figure, Philip K. Dick, who breaks both of those molds and creates something arguably much more painful.

30:10.480 --> 30:14.120
Philip K. Dick represents the third distinct behavior in this taxonomy.

30:14.120 --> 30:23.260
Dick was, of course, one of the most prolific and influential science fiction writers of the 20th century, works like Blade Runner, the man in the high castle minority report.

30:23.520 --> 30:28.780
He was a master of exploring paranoia, simulated realities, and the breakdown of identity.

30:29.020 --> 30:32.060
But the fiction bled into reality for him in a massive way.

30:32.560 --> 30:40.900
In February and March of 1974, recovering from dental surgery and dealing with a lot of personal stress, Dick experienced a series of profound, anomalous events.

30:41.020 --> 30:43.120
This is the famous 2374 experience.

30:43.120 --> 30:47.500
Yes, it famously started when a delivery woman brought his pain medication.

30:47.860 --> 30:50.780
He noticed she was wearing a necklace with a Christian fish symbol.

30:51.340 --> 30:56.860
When the sun caught the goldfish, Dick experienced what he described as a pink beam of light hitting his eyes.

30:57.200 --> 30:59.280
And this beam didn't just blind him.

30:59.800 --> 31:03.900
He felt it downloaded an immense amount of information directly into his mind.

31:04.340 --> 31:09.440
He felt he suddenly remembered that he was a persecuted Christian living in ancient Rome in the first century.

31:09.440 --> 31:20.140
He became convinced that the Roman Empire had never actually ended, that history had been frozen, and that 1974 California was just a holographic illusion overlaying the reality of the oppressive Roman state.

31:20.200 --> 31:22.820
Which is a terrifying thing to genuinely believe.

31:22.920 --> 31:24.060
And it didn't stop there.

31:24.620 --> 31:27.820
Over the next two months, he experienced intense hallucinations.

31:27.820 --> 31:40.960
He felt his mind was taken over by a rational alien intelligence he later called vast, vast, active living intelligence system, which actually helped him diagnose an illness in his son that doctors had missed.

31:40.960 --> 31:43.460
It was an overwhelming ontological shock.

31:44.200 --> 31:46.700
Reality as he knew it just completely shattered.

31:46.700 --> 31:52.960
And he spent the rest of his life, until his death in 1982, trying to understand what happened to him during those two months.

31:53.240 --> 31:56.180
And his attempt to understand it became the exegesis.

31:56.880 --> 31:59.400
This is an absolutely staggering document.

31:59.980 --> 32:09.240
We're talking about an obsessive, late-night, manic journaling process that resulted in roughly 8,000 pages of handwritten and typed notes.

32:09.280 --> 32:10.280
8,000 pages.

32:10.520 --> 32:11.560
That's unbelievable.

32:11.560 --> 32:20.940
He is applying our Roe operator recontextualizing, cross-referencing, theorizing, to this single founding anomaly from 1974.

32:21.360 --> 32:25.800
But unlike Luhmann, the exegesis does not just freely diverge into unrelated topics.

32:26.020 --> 32:27.940
He doesn't start writing about contract law.

32:28.440 --> 32:31.520
He is obsessively, desperately tethered to that one event.

32:31.680 --> 32:31.820
Right.

32:31.880 --> 32:33.360
He never leaves the pink beam behind.

32:33.620 --> 32:37.780
And unlike Swedenborg, it never converges into a stable, fixed cosmology.

32:37.780 --> 32:41.480
He never settles on a final answer and says, aha, I figured it out.

32:41.580 --> 32:42.120
It was God.

32:42.320 --> 32:45.820
The paper describes this third state as a constant cycle of reconstruction.

32:46.460 --> 32:48.960
It is a perpetual motion machine of anxiety.

32:49.100 --> 32:50.740
It's like, okay, let me try this analogy.

32:51.020 --> 32:53.600
It's like he's trying to paint a still life of an apple.

32:53.820 --> 32:54.480
Okay, an apple.

32:54.700 --> 32:56.180
He looks at the apple, he paints it.

32:56.540 --> 33:00.980
But the sheer act of looking at his painting, of applying that interpretation to reality,

33:01.440 --> 33:04.980
magically turns the real apple sitting on the table into an orange.

33:05.260 --> 33:06.260
Oh, wow.

33:06.260 --> 33:07.780
So his painting is now wrong.

33:08.180 --> 33:10.760
He has to throw away the painting and start frantically painting an orange.

33:11.060 --> 33:15.760
But as he finishes the orange, the act of interpreting it turns the real fruit into a banana.

33:16.280 --> 33:18.140
He has to start all over again constantly.

33:18.500 --> 33:20.080
The target is never where he left it.

33:20.360 --> 33:24.200
That analogy captures the brutal mechanics of his situation perfectly.

33:25.160 --> 33:30.060
In Appendix C, the paper defines this by introducing the concept of endogenous objects.

33:30.460 --> 33:31.420
Endogenous objects.

33:31.420 --> 33:31.940
Yes.

33:32.300 --> 33:38.780
In a normal interpretive problem, say a historian studying a battle, the object you are studying is exogenous.

33:39.380 --> 33:42.200
It sits outside of you, fixed and unchanging in the past.

33:42.580 --> 33:45.820
Your theory about the battle doesn't travel back in time and change the battle.

33:45.940 --> 33:47.420
But Dick's object was endogenous.

33:47.980 --> 33:49.160
It was inside the system.

33:49.160 --> 34:07.340
Because every time he applied an interpretation to the 1974 event, let's say he spent a week writing 100 pages hypothesizing that the pink beam was a Soviet telepathic weapon, the intense obsessive act of writing that down, of fully entertaining that hypothesis, would generate psychological effects.

34:07.340 --> 34:08.740
It would feed back into his brain.

34:08.940 --> 34:09.420
Exactly.

34:09.680 --> 34:10.800
It would trigger new dreams.

34:10.940 --> 34:16.200
It would cause him to reframe old memories from his childhood in a new light to fit the Soviet theory.

34:16.420 --> 34:24.180
It would make him hypersensitive to coincidences in his daily life, interpreting a wrong number on the phone as a KGB check-in.

34:24.380 --> 34:24.760
Wow.

34:25.100 --> 34:29.060
So the act of interpreting creates new, bizarre data points.

34:29.060 --> 34:38.380
And those new data points then had to be archived and interpreted, which meant the original theory, that it was a Soviet weapon, was no longer sufficient to explain this new influx of data.

34:38.640 --> 34:41.040
He had to abandon it and build a new theory.

34:41.160 --> 34:42.740
Maybe it's an extraterrestrial satellite.

34:43.040 --> 34:46.800
But writing about the satellite generated new dreams, and the cycle continues.

34:47.400 --> 34:52.480
The interpretation changed what he believed the original 1974 experience actually was.

34:52.640 --> 34:55.700
It is an inescapable recursive feedback loop.

34:55.700 --> 35:01.760
The interpretation creates new data, and the new data changes the event being interpreted.

35:02.120 --> 35:03.880
The founding event is not a fixed input.

35:04.240 --> 35:08.560
It is constituted anew by every single attempt to fix it in place.

35:08.900 --> 35:15.820
The archive is not converging into a neat religion, and it is not freely diverging into productive academic surprise.

35:16.040 --> 35:20.700
It is continually reconstituting the very object it takes itself to be interpreting.

35:20.700 --> 35:27.460
As the paper says, it is a machine for perpetuating a problem that its own operation prevents from ever being solved.

35:27.580 --> 35:35.020
That sounds like a description of absolute hell, to be forced to constantly solve a puzzle where the pieces change shape every time you touch them.

35:35.080 --> 35:37.660
Okay, so that completes the first massive phase of this paper.

35:38.080 --> 35:41.500
We've built a solid taxonomy of three distinct archival behaviors.

35:41.640 --> 35:41.820
Right.

35:41.820 --> 35:47.060
First, sustained difference and divergence, which we saw with Lumen's slipbox generating new ideas.

35:47.360 --> 35:52.660
Second, a stabilized cosmology, the gravity well we saw with Swedenborg's angels.

35:53.240 --> 35:58.920
And third, the continual reconstitution of the target, the feedback loop that trapped Philip K. Dick.

35:59.400 --> 36:05.080
But right here, roughly halfway through the deep dive, the paper executes this incredible pivot.

36:05.080 --> 36:10.180
It steps back and points out a massive blind spot in everything we've discussed so far.

36:10.240 --> 36:11.200
The blind spot.

36:11.280 --> 36:11.720
Yes.

36:12.160 --> 36:23.260
The taxonomy we just built is entirely accurate for those three men, but it turns out that Lumen, Swedenborg, and Dick all took three massive foundational assumptions completely for granted.

36:23.480 --> 36:26.100
They were operating inside a box they didn't even know existed.

36:26.360 --> 36:30.280
They all assumed three things about how knowledge works without ever questioning them.

36:30.280 --> 36:35.320
First, they assumed that the interpreter's past thoughts always feed forward into their next thought.

36:36.000 --> 36:38.280
Your past notes always influence your future notes.

36:38.800 --> 36:40.000
You carry your baggage with you.

36:40.280 --> 36:45.240
Second, they assumed the archive is only answerable to its own internal logic.

36:45.380 --> 36:47.360
It doesn't have to check in with the outside world to see if it's true.

36:47.820 --> 36:53.240
And third, they assumed that the world they are interpreting is a fixed, given reality.

36:53.600 --> 36:56.480
It's just the world sitting out there waiting to be understood.

36:56.480 --> 37:01.500
And this is where FLYXion introduces what I consider the master stroke of the essay.

37:01.780 --> 37:04.380
In Appendix G, the author introduces the general model.

37:04.840 --> 37:08.380
It's the overarching equation of this entire deep dive.

37:08.720 --> 37:10.180
Okay, let's break down this equation.

37:10.420 --> 37:11.400
How does it actually work?

37:11.760 --> 37:18.380
The model states that the update to our cognitive state, the next thought we have, is a function of four interacting things.

37:18.520 --> 37:19.280
Four variables.

37:19.280 --> 37:28.940
S. First, alpha. This represents the degree to which the interpreter's own prior state, their ongoing internal monologue, is carried forward into the next moment.

37:29.000 --> 37:29.400
The baggage.

37:29.640 --> 37:29.980
Exactly.

37:30.720 --> 37:35.440
Second, A. This is the accumulated archive we've been discussing, the slip box or the diary.

37:36.180 --> 37:40.840
Third, E. The external constraint or independent evidence from the physical universe.

37:41.520 --> 37:47.980
And fourth, W. The constructed space of possibility or the world in which the interpretation is occurring.

37:47.980 --> 37:54.480
So to put it simply, Lumen, Swedenborg, and Dick were obsessed with tweaking variable A, the archive.

37:54.900 --> 37:56.500
They built crazy filing systems.

37:57.040 --> 38:01.060
But they left alpha, E, and W entirely alone.

38:01.220 --> 38:04.200
They just accepted the default settings of being a human being.

38:04.520 --> 38:05.000
Exactly.

38:05.280 --> 38:10.720
Which means, if we want to see what those other variables do, if we want to see the full spectrum of how knowledge is built,

38:10.720 --> 38:17.900
we have to look at three completely different historical figures who actively manipulated those hidden variables.

38:17.900 --> 38:20.160
This brings us to the second half of our journey.

38:20.800 --> 38:24.580
Jeanne Guillon, Cecilia Pingepoškin, and Ursula K. Leguan.

38:25.080 --> 38:26.960
Let's dive right into Jeanne Guillon.

38:27.500 --> 38:29.380
She is manipulating variable alpha.

38:29.860 --> 38:30.480
Who was she?

38:30.960 --> 38:35.440
Jeanne Guillon was a prominent and highly controversial 17th century French mystic.

38:35.780 --> 38:38.580
She was a central figure in a movement known as quietism.

38:39.040 --> 38:45.080
She wrote a massively influential book called Moyen Court, which translates to a short and easy method of prayer.

38:45.080 --> 38:47.540
And she got into a lot of trouble for this book, didn't she?

38:47.900 --> 38:48.620
Immense trouble.

38:49.000 --> 38:55.900
She was imprisoned in the Bastille by order of the king and the church because her ideas were seen as deeply threatening to the religious establishment.

38:56.280 --> 38:57.600
What was so threatening about it?

38:57.860 --> 39:01.480
Her whole approach was centered on something she called the prayer of quiet.

39:02.380 --> 39:07.820
When faced with the overwhelming nature of the divine or a massive anomalous spiritual experience,

39:08.060 --> 39:13.340
the same kind of overwhelming anomaly that drove Swedenborg and Dick to write thousands of pages,

39:13.340 --> 39:17.380
is Guillon did not build a massive cross-referenced filing system.

39:18.120 --> 39:20.120
Her response was the complete opposite.

39:20.680 --> 39:26.080
Her response was the deliberate, disciplined relinquishing of discursive elaboration.

39:26.220 --> 39:27.640
What did that actually mean in practice?

39:28.080 --> 39:31.180
Discursive elaboration is what you and I are doing right now.

39:31.460 --> 39:33.600
It is the mind's constant churning.

39:33.600 --> 39:41.260
It is the desire to construct images, arguments, complex theories, and sequential narratives about what we are experiencing.

39:41.480 --> 39:42.780
Just constantly talking to yourself.

39:42.940 --> 39:43.120
Right.

39:43.460 --> 39:48.260
Guillon practiced a form of contemplation where, when an overwhelming spiritual sensation occurred,

39:48.700 --> 39:54.460
she actively silenced the mind's attempt to categorize it, name it, or file it away for later use.

39:54.600 --> 39:55.820
Wait, I have to challenge this.

39:55.820 --> 40:02.320
I get the spiritual appeal of silence, but in the context of our deep dive about archives and information processing,

40:02.540 --> 40:04.320
how does doing nothing fit in?

40:04.420 --> 40:05.480
It's not doing nothing.

40:05.580 --> 40:12.000
But she's hit with an overwhelming experience, and her response is to refuse to file it, refuse to write it down,

40:12.120 --> 40:13.440
refuse to think about it logically.

40:14.020 --> 40:15.940
Isn't that just the absence of a system?

40:16.220 --> 40:18.480
How is she manipulating a variable?

40:18.600 --> 40:25.420
It looks like nothing from the outside, but formally, structurally, it is a highly active, difficult process.

40:25.420 --> 40:30.960
Guillon is deliberately manipulating our first hidden variable, alpha.

40:31.540 --> 40:35.280
She is driving the interpreter's carryover variable towards zero.

40:35.620 --> 40:39.460
She is refusing to let her past self dictate her present moment.

40:39.660 --> 40:40.180
Precisely.

40:40.320 --> 40:45.120
She recognized that the interpreter's own accumulated thoughts, their preconceptions, their anxieties,

40:45.280 --> 40:50.820
their desperate need to figure it all out, can become the biggest obstacle to actually perceiving reality.

40:50.960 --> 40:51.280
Wow.

40:51.280 --> 40:55.700
By attenuating the self, by refusing to feed her past thoughts into her present moment,

40:55.960 --> 40:58.600
she is deliberately breaking the recursive feedback loop.

40:58.660 --> 40:58.900
Oh.

40:59.700 --> 41:04.960
She is preventing exactly the kind of infinite reconstructive loop that trapped Philip K. Dick.

41:05.340 --> 41:08.060
Dick couldn't stop feeding his own theories back into his visions.

41:08.580 --> 41:10.080
Guillon just stops the conveyor belt.

41:10.400 --> 41:17.060
She is actively starving the engine of its fuel so it doesn't spin out of control into a manic exegesis.

41:17.060 --> 41:18.060
That is profound.

41:18.960 --> 41:24.160
And the paper makes a stunning observation here, connecting this deeply religious mystic practice

41:24.160 --> 41:27.820
to the most secular, logical movements in history.

41:28.000 --> 41:30.820
This is one of those connections that just makes you sit back in your chair.

41:31.360 --> 41:37.660
The paper points out that this 17th century mystic practice anticipates, in its pure formal structure,

41:38.240 --> 41:42.620
the work of highly secular mathematicians and philosophers centuries later.

41:42.800 --> 41:44.760
Which seems impossible at first glance.

41:44.760 --> 41:46.840
Consider George Boole in the 19th century.

41:47.440 --> 41:52.600
He invented Boolean algebra, the binary logic of true and false that is the literal foundation

41:52.600 --> 41:56.300
of all modern computing and the device you are listening to this on right now.

41:56.380 --> 41:57.400
Right, the zeros and ones.

41:57.760 --> 42:02.220
Boole's entire project was to represent the laws of thought as a mathematical algebra

42:02.220 --> 42:08.200
that did not depend on any individual human reasoner's discretion, mood, or psychological baggage.

42:08.620 --> 42:12.580
Or think of the logical positivists in the 1920s, the Vienna Circle.

42:12.580 --> 42:14.420
Yes, figures like Carnap.

42:15.060 --> 42:20.080
They insisted that a statement only had meaning if it could be verified logically or empirically,

42:20.380 --> 42:25.240
completely independently of the interpreter's own subjective elaboration or metaphysical feelings.

42:25.460 --> 42:31.540
What Efeleixion points out is that Boole, the logical positivists, and Jane Guion all share

42:31.540 --> 42:34.880
the exact same structural strategy for dealing with overwhelming complexity.

42:34.880 --> 42:40.020
They are all achieving reliability by removing the contamination of the individual interpreter's

42:40.020 --> 42:41.580
ongoing recursive thoughts.

42:41.980 --> 42:43.940
They are all driving alpha to zero.

42:44.360 --> 42:49.480
They just applied it to different domains, mysticism, mathematics, and philosophy of science.

42:49.480 --> 42:50.980
That is a wild connection.

42:51.520 --> 42:56.960
From 17th century French mystics praying in silence to the logic gates processing ones and zeros

42:56.960 --> 42:57.740
in our smartphones.

42:58.300 --> 43:01.700
Both are attempting to find truth by muting the noisy human ego.

43:01.700 --> 43:05.260
It really reframes how you look at both religion and science.

43:05.440 --> 43:08.340
Okay, let's move to our next variable and our next figure.

43:08.520 --> 43:10.380
We've seen what happens when you silence the cell.

43:10.600 --> 43:14.100
Now we look at what happens when you force your archive to listen to the universe.

43:14.520 --> 43:17.020
We are looking at variable E external constraint.

43:17.500 --> 43:20.580
And the figure here is Cecilia Payne-Gaposhkin.

43:20.980 --> 43:26.060
Cecilia Payne-Gaposhkin is a towering figure in the history of science, though she rarely gets

43:26.060 --> 43:27.780
the public recognition she deserves.

43:27.780 --> 43:34.120
In 1925, she was a young woman working on her doctoral dissertation at the Harvard College

43:34.120 --> 43:34.840
Observatory.

43:35.440 --> 43:38.280
Her focus was analyzing stellar spectra.

43:38.780 --> 43:39.620
Stellar spectra.

43:39.740 --> 43:44.900
This is taking the light coming from a distant star, passing it through a prism, and looking

43:44.900 --> 43:49.840
at the resulting barcode of colors and dark lines to figure out what the star is made of,

43:49.960 --> 43:50.160
right?

43:50.420 --> 43:50.840
Exactly.

43:51.320 --> 43:56.340
Every chemical element absorbs light at specific wavelengths, leaving a unique fingerprint of

43:56.340 --> 43:57.660
dark lines on the spectrum.

43:58.380 --> 44:02.780
But analyzing these lines was incredibly difficult because a star's temperature changes how the

44:02.780 --> 44:03.660
elements behave.

44:03.880 --> 44:05.760
So it wasn't just a simple one-to-one mapping.

44:05.940 --> 44:06.180
Right.

44:06.700 --> 44:11.300
Payne-Gaposhkin applied the newly developed physics of thermal ionization, specifically

44:11.300 --> 44:14.140
Meghnot-Saha's theory, to these stellar barcodes.

44:14.220 --> 44:15.760
I promise we wouldn't just throw a jargon around.

44:15.840 --> 44:19.200
So let's explain Meghnot-Saha's theory of thermal ionization.

44:19.300 --> 44:19.920
How does it work?

44:20.180 --> 44:21.900
Think about boiling a pot of water.

44:21.900 --> 44:26.940
As you add heat, the water molecules get excited and change state from liquid to gas.

44:27.320 --> 44:31.380
Thermal ionization is somewhat similar, but on an atomic level.

44:31.860 --> 44:35.460
An atom consists of a nucleus surrounded by a cloud of electrons.

44:36.140 --> 44:40.620
If you heat a gas to extreme temperatures, like the temperatures found in the atmosphere

44:40.620 --> 44:45.600
of a star, the collisions between atoms become so violent that electrons get knocked

44:45.600 --> 44:46.660
right off their orbits.

44:46.760 --> 44:48.900
So the atom loses an electron and becomes an ion.

44:48.900 --> 44:49.580
Right.

44:49.840 --> 44:55.280
And Saha's equation allowed astrophysicists to calculate exactly what percentage of atoms

44:55.280 --> 44:59.760
in a star's atmosphere would be ionized at any given temperature and pressure.

45:00.020 --> 45:02.480
That's a huge missing piece of the puzzle.

45:02.820 --> 45:07.740
Payne-Gaposhkin realized that the presence or absence of dark lines in a star's spectrum

45:07.740 --> 45:09.940
wasn't just about what elements were there.

45:10.200 --> 45:11.920
It was about how hot the star was.

45:12.360 --> 45:16.660
By using Saha's equation to account for the temperature, she was able to calculate the

45:16.660 --> 45:19.420
actual underlying abundance of the elements.

45:19.540 --> 45:21.480
And her calculations proved something revolutionary.

45:22.240 --> 45:27.480
She proved definitively that stars are overwhelmingly made of hydrogen and helium.

45:27.920 --> 45:32.320
Which is literally the foundation of all modern stellar astrophysics.

45:32.600 --> 45:37.320
But the story of what happened when she made this discovery is incredible, and it perfectly

45:37.320 --> 45:39.260
illustrates the friction of variable E.

45:39.260 --> 45:44.200
When she was preparing to publish her dissertation, she shared her findings with Henry Norris Russell.

45:45.080 --> 45:50.020
Russell was the director of the Princeton University Observatory and basically the ultimate authority,

45:50.140 --> 45:52.540
the undisputed king of American astronomy at the time.

45:52.760 --> 45:55.760
And what was the prevailing wisdom that Russell enforced?

45:56.300 --> 46:00.400
Well, the accepted scientific dogma of the 1920s was that the universe was uniform.

46:01.160 --> 46:06.360
They believed that stars had roughly the same elemental composition as the crust of the Earth.

46:06.360 --> 46:10.020
Mostly heavy elements like iron, silicon, and oxygen.

46:10.540 --> 46:15.420
So when Pyn Kapashkin handed Russell her math, showing that stars were almost entirely hydrogen,

46:15.680 --> 46:19.140
millions of times more hydrogen than the Earth, he told her it was impossible.

46:19.720 --> 46:24.500
He told her the conclusion was clearly flawed because it flew in the face of everything they knew.

46:24.840 --> 46:30.100
And this young female doctoral student, facing down the most powerful man in her field,

46:30.700 --> 46:31.580
had to make a choice.

46:31.880 --> 46:32.460
What did she do?

46:32.460 --> 46:36.060
She actually appended a note to her own groundbreaking findings.

46:36.700 --> 46:41.940
In her published thesis, after laying out all the flawless math proving the hydrogen abundance,

46:42.480 --> 46:47.900
she wrote that the calculated abundances of hydrogen and helium were almost certainly not real,

46:48.300 --> 46:51.360
and probably the result of some unknown flaw in the theory.

46:51.780 --> 46:52.920
She doubted herself.

46:53.480 --> 46:56.280
She subordinated her own perfect math to his opinion.

46:56.860 --> 47:00.380
But how does this relate to the archive and our deep dive?

47:00.380 --> 47:07.580
This story perfectly illustrates how variable E, external constraint, actually functions in the real world.

47:08.760 --> 47:15.220
Unlike Swedenborg's archive, which could invent detailed societies on Jupiter without ever being checked by a telescope,

47:16.000 --> 47:21.280
Pyn Kapashkin's archive of calculations was answerable to a physical universe outside of her own head.

47:21.680 --> 47:26.320
Her math had to align with the actual photons of light hitting the photographic plates at Harvard.

47:26.320 --> 47:30.620
Exactly. But if her math was tied to reality, why did she back down?

47:30.760 --> 47:32.600
Because it complicates the model beautifully.

47:33.100 --> 47:38.980
Giving authority to an external constraint, variable E, doesn't just mean passively listening to the physical universe.

47:39.140 --> 47:40.440
The universe doesn't speak English.

47:40.780 --> 47:48.200
Right. In human practice, listening to the universe almost always means wrestling with socially authoritative interpretations of that universe.

47:48.200 --> 47:52.300
Henry Norris Russell acted as the social proxy for reality.

47:52.540 --> 47:53.400
He was the gatekeeper.

47:53.400 --> 47:57.400
Her archive, her math, was perfectly aligned with physical reality.

47:57.980 --> 48:02.820
But she temporarily subordinated that truth to a flawed social authority.

48:03.180 --> 48:09.100
I read that a few years later, Russell actually did his own independent calculations using a different method,

48:09.600 --> 48:15.160
realized she had been right all along, and published a paper confirming the overwhelming abundance of hydrogen.

48:15.160 --> 48:19.500
He did. He gave her credit, but it just shows how messy the E variable can be.

48:19.920 --> 48:22.180
Truth has to fight through the social filter.

48:22.480 --> 48:22.860
It does.

48:23.560 --> 48:27.320
But ultimately, her case proves the long-term power of the E variable.

48:27.880 --> 48:32.720
In a healthy knowledge system tied to reality, some interpretations are eventually forced out.

48:33.140 --> 48:35.880
Not because the archive can't accommodate them internally,

48:36.020 --> 48:39.780
the Earth composition theory of stars was internally consistent for a long time,

48:39.900 --> 48:43.980
but because the physical universe simply won't tolerate the wrong answer indefinitely.

48:43.980 --> 48:48.900
The external evidence acts as an anchor, preventing the archive from floating off

48:48.900 --> 48:52.660
into a beautifully consistent but entirely fictional paracosm.

48:53.020 --> 48:54.340
The universe pushes back.

48:54.500 --> 48:57.900
If you are wrong about a star, the star's light will eventually prove you wrong,

48:58.040 --> 48:59.680
no matter how elegant your math is.

48:59.940 --> 49:02.660
Which brings us to our final figure and our final variable,

49:03.200 --> 49:06.160
W, the constraint space, or the constructed world.

49:06.620 --> 49:11.880
And for this, the paper turns to one of my absolute favorite authors, Ursula K. Le Guin.

49:11.880 --> 49:15.100
Le Guin is entirely unique in this lineup.

49:15.460 --> 49:17.580
She is manipulating the environment itself.

49:18.040 --> 49:22.560
She is neither withdrawing from archival elaboration like Guillaume driving Alpha to Zero,

49:22.860 --> 49:27.560
nor is she submitting to external physical evidence like Panga Poshkin leaning on E.

49:27.800 --> 49:34.360
She is deliberately, consciously constructing the space, the world, with which interpretation occurs.

49:34.360 --> 49:40.560
Le Guin was a titan of science fiction and fantasy, famous for Earthsea, the dispossessed, and the left hand of darkness.

49:40.960 --> 49:42.580
And her background is vital here.

49:42.820 --> 49:44.720
Her parents were prominent anthropologists.

49:45.120 --> 49:49.420
She grew up surrounded by the study of diverse human cultures, myths, and social structures.

49:49.420 --> 49:54.380
The paper highlights her carrier bag theory of fiction, which is an essay she wrote in 1986.

49:55.300 --> 49:59.220
It's this brilliant, paradigm-shifting idea about storytelling.

50:00.180 --> 50:03.040
She argues that we've been conditioned to view narrative as a weapon,

50:03.480 --> 50:07.500
like a spear thrusting linearly toward a single violent climactic resolution.

50:08.440 --> 50:09.680
The hero kills the mammoth.

50:09.980 --> 50:10.640
End of story.

50:10.640 --> 50:12.760
But Le Guin proposes an alternative.

50:13.400 --> 50:15.940
She argues that the first human tool wasn't a spear.

50:16.300 --> 50:20.260
It was a receptacle, a carrier bag to gather oats, berries, and roots.

50:20.520 --> 50:22.720
A completely different way of looking at survival.

50:23.120 --> 50:25.200
A narrative, therefore, shouldn't be a spear.

50:25.300 --> 50:27.420
It should be a container, a carrier bag,

50:27.600 --> 50:32.480
built to hold a vast, heterogeneous plurality of experiences, voices, and unresolved conflicts

50:32.480 --> 50:36.380
without demanding that they all resolve into one neat, victorious answer.

50:36.380 --> 50:40.400
How does the carrier bag theory connect to our model of the archive in Variable W?

50:40.640 --> 50:44.060
A novel, in Le Guin's hands, acts as a simulated archive.

50:44.660 --> 50:46.360
She takes the variable W, the world,

50:46.480 --> 50:49.580
which all our other figures treat as a fixed, invisible background reality,

50:49.820 --> 50:52.240
and she turns it into an experimental variable.

50:52.760 --> 50:56.960
She treats the laws of society, physics, and biology as dials she can turn.

50:57.460 --> 50:59.100
In her masterpiece, The Dispossessed,

50:59.640 --> 51:03.220
she constructs two radically different planetary societies.

51:03.560 --> 51:09.120
One is Xenares, a harsh, resource-poor anarchist collective where property is theft.

51:09.120 --> 51:15.880
The other is Uras, a wealthy, propertied, highly stratified capitalist state that looks a lot like Earth.

51:16.220 --> 51:19.460
She traces a physicist, Shevek, moving between them.

51:20.240 --> 51:22.980
She is using the novel to see how human subjectivity,

51:23.340 --> 51:24.840
how the archive of a human mind,

51:25.280 --> 51:27.820
changes based on the constraints of the world it inhabits.

51:27.960 --> 51:30.020
So she isn't archiving notes about the world.

51:30.100 --> 51:34.800
She's archiving entire alternate worlds to see how human behavior changes inside them.

51:34.800 --> 51:36.680
I think of it like computer hardware and software.

51:37.500 --> 51:41.560
Swedenborg thought the software of human nature was hard-coded into the hardware of the universe.

51:42.180 --> 51:44.380
Le Guin is running completely different operating systems.

51:44.940 --> 51:47.260
Anarchism, capitalism, different gender structures,

51:47.540 --> 51:51.940
on the exact same human hardware to prove that the software is entirely malleable.

51:52.160 --> 51:53.840
That is a phenomenal analogy.

51:54.360 --> 51:57.120
By placing human beings inside different operating systems,

51:57.260 --> 51:59.520
she proves a massive philosophical point.

52:00.020 --> 52:01.780
Take her novel, The Left Hand of Darkness.

52:02.060 --> 52:03.180
Oh, this is a great example.

52:03.180 --> 52:07.860
She constructs a society on the ice planet Geffen where individuals are ambisexual.

52:08.620 --> 52:11.940
They are without fixed biological sex for most of their monthly cycle,

52:12.420 --> 52:16.940
only interrupting male or female characteristics during a brief reproductive period.

52:17.160 --> 52:19.900
There are no permanent men or women in that society.

52:20.240 --> 52:23.140
By manipulating that single variable in her constructed world,

52:23.320 --> 52:25.740
she forces the reader to confront their own world.

52:26.440 --> 52:32.160
She forces us to realize that categories we treat as biological givens or background natural laws,

52:32.160 --> 52:38.380
how we view power, war, love, and identity are actually highly contingent built arrangements.

52:39.220 --> 52:43.720
Where Swedenborg thought he was discovering a pre-existing given spiritual world with his archive,

52:44.140 --> 52:47.460
Le Guin knowingly constructs constraint spaces purely to test them.

52:48.100 --> 52:50.920
She shows that identity, gender, cultural knowledge,

52:50.920 --> 52:52.940
all the things we assume are just reality,

52:53.420 --> 52:57.380
are actually artifacts of whichever constructed world happens to be holding them in place.

52:57.760 --> 52:59.260
The world itself is a variable.

52:59.480 --> 53:02.340
And that completes the general model introduced by FLY Zion.

53:02.740 --> 53:06.120
To understand an archive, to understand how humans process information,

53:06.480 --> 53:08.900
we cannot just look at the filing system, variable A.

53:08.900 --> 53:11.760
We have to look at the entirely coupled system.

53:12.360 --> 53:16.140
The archive itself, the interpreter's psychological carryover, which is Alpha,

53:16.520 --> 53:19.960
the external evidence tying it to reality, which is E,

53:20.240 --> 53:24.840
and the specific world space in which the entire process is unfolding, which is W.

53:25.540 --> 53:29.200
Okay, let's take a breath and bring all of this massive theory together.

53:29.540 --> 53:31.960
We have covered an immense amount of ground today.

53:31.960 --> 53:33.060
We really have.

53:33.240 --> 53:37.100
We've gone from medieval monks indexing scripture in a floriligium

53:37.100 --> 53:42.340
to a 20th century sociologist obsessively numbering 90,000 note cards.

53:42.480 --> 53:46.180
We've gone from an 18th century scientist having visions of angels on Jupiter

53:46.180 --> 53:51.660
to a 1970s sci-fi author trapped in a paranoid feedback loop of pink lasers.

53:52.040 --> 53:54.120
We've looked at mystic silence in a French prison,

53:54.480 --> 53:56.860
the thermodynamics of stellar astrophysics at Harvard,

53:57.240 --> 53:59.980
and the anarchist utopias of modern science fiction.

53:59.980 --> 54:04.880
It is a dizzying journey, but through it all, a single unifying thread has emerged

54:04.880 --> 54:09.320
about what an archive truly is and what happens when we outsource our minds.

54:09.460 --> 54:10.900
Archiving is not about storage.

54:11.380 --> 54:12.960
A locked drawer stores things.

54:13.460 --> 54:15.400
A computer hard drive stores things.

54:15.680 --> 54:19.080
Archiving, whether you are doing it with index cards, spiritual diaries,

54:19.320 --> 54:22.900
logical mathematical reduction, or world-building science fiction novels,

54:23.080 --> 54:25.760
is a dynamic coupled system.

54:25.940 --> 54:27.280
It is a time machine of sorts.

54:27.280 --> 54:30.880
It is a highly engineered way for a future version of yourself

54:30.880 --> 54:34.140
to encounter your past self, external reality,

54:34.460 --> 54:38.820
or an alternate world under conditions that simply did not exist before.

54:39.240 --> 54:42.960
Which brings us, finally, to the title of F.L. Weigstein's essay,

54:43.460 --> 54:44.940
Perichosms of the Strange.

54:45.360 --> 54:48.620
The paper ends with a formal definition of the filed perichosm.

54:49.140 --> 54:52.820
A perichosm is a detailed imaginary world usually created by a child.

54:52.820 --> 54:56.200
But these figures, Lumen, Swedenborg, Dick,

54:56.540 --> 54:59.520
they didn't just passively live in private, complex worlds.

54:59.620 --> 55:00.040
They filed?

55:00.200 --> 55:04.760
They built physical apparatuses, addressing schemes, slip boxes, and 8,000-page exeguses

55:04.760 --> 55:07.580
to allow them to return to worlds that were simply too vast,

55:07.680 --> 55:12.320
too overwhelming, and too strange for their frail, unassisted human memories to hold still.

55:12.500 --> 55:14.800
The apparatus itself kept generating the world.

55:15.000 --> 55:17.420
The filing system was the engine of their reality.

55:17.420 --> 55:21.520
That is the beautiful and somewhat unsettling truth of the essay.

55:22.540 --> 55:26.500
The vividness, the scale, and the coherence of the worlds they lived in

55:26.500 --> 55:29.340
depended less on the raw experiences they had

55:29.340 --> 55:32.860
and much more on the physical and mathematical architecture they built

55:32.860 --> 55:34.340
to keep returning to them.

55:34.460 --> 55:37.280
Which leaves us with a question for you listening to this right now.

55:37.700 --> 55:41.600
We started by talking about the overwhelming feeling of having too many tabs open.

55:41.900 --> 55:44.380
Think about your own digital archives today.

55:44.380 --> 55:49.060
Think about your browser bookmarks, the thousands of photos in your camera roll,

55:49.160 --> 55:51.860
your notion boards, your obsidian vaults.

55:52.040 --> 55:56.440
Think about the algorithms on TikTok or YouTube that literally feed you content

55:56.440 --> 56:01.680
based on your past clicks, constantly recontextualizing your own interests back to you.

56:01.760 --> 56:04.960
When you retrieve a memory, a note, or a video from your phone,

56:05.180 --> 56:07.140
what is that architecture doing to your mind?

56:07.420 --> 56:12.060
Are you using it like Nicholas Lumen to generate new, productive,

56:12.060 --> 56:14.520
endless surprises that push your thinking forward?

56:14.900 --> 56:16.420
Are you using it like Emanuel Swedenborg,

56:16.860 --> 56:19.140
carefully stabilizing a personal mythology,

56:19.680 --> 56:23.060
curating an echo chamber that perfectly reinforces a completely

56:23.060 --> 56:26.520
uncheckable version of your own political or social reality?

56:26.860 --> 56:29.620
Or are you caught in a loop like Philip K. Dick,

56:29.980 --> 56:32.300
constantly reconstructing your own identity,

56:32.840 --> 56:35.620
endlessly painting an apple that turns into an orange,

56:36.040 --> 56:39.140
based on whatever the algorithm decides to feed back to you

56:39.140 --> 56:41.820
in an inescapable feedback loop of anxiety?

56:42.440 --> 56:44.520
It is a profound question of modern life.

56:45.080 --> 56:47.620
We think we are just building tools to hold our thoughts,

56:48.260 --> 56:52.460
but we have to ask how the systems we build end up building us in return.

56:52.700 --> 56:54.860
When you have too many tabs open in your brain,

56:55.400 --> 56:58.680
the system you build to hold the overflow doesn't just catch your thoughts,

56:58.880 --> 57:00.520
it starts thinking with you.

57:00.640 --> 57:02.840
And you better make sure you know what kind of machine you build.

57:02.840 --> 57:05.460
Thank you for taking this deep dive with us today.

57:05.560 --> 57:08.400
It's been an absolute trip through the architecture of the human mind.

57:08.600 --> 57:09.800
We'll catch you on the next one.

